                <h2>Weekly Compliance Alert: Three Types of Operations Triggered Mass Bans</h2>
<p>The Yiyu Digital Media compliance audit team received 8 account abnormality reports from clients over the past week. After initial investigation, problems were highly concentrated in three types of operations. </p>
<p><strong>Type 1: Sudden Payment Channel Change</strong></p>
<p>A utility app team had their Meta ad account suspended for nearly 48 hours after changing their payment credit card mid-week. The account triggered Meta's security review within 4 hours of the change. This team had never encountered this issue before.</p>
<p>This is not an isolated case. Meta strengthened its real-time monitoring logic for payment method changes this week. Newly linked cards with significant discrepancies from the account's historical spending records (e.g., card region inconsistent with account registration region) will automatically enter a manual review queue. It is recommended to update qualifications in the business backend before changing payment methods.</p>
<p><strong>Type 2: Overly Precise Audience Targeting</strong></p>
<p>A cross-border e-commerce team focusing on the Middle East market found their Google Ads account's audience size dropped sharply to under 200 people this week, leaving ads with almost no display opportunities. Investigation revealed they stacked age, interests, behaviors, and geographic location settings to form an extremely narrow audience pack to pursue high ROI.</p>
<p>Google's advertising policy explicitly states that exploiting system loopholes or artificially compressing audiences to circumvent reviews is a high-risk operation. If your audience size has been consistently below 1,000 people, it is recommended to immediately broaden targeting conditions.</p>
<p><strong>Type 3: Highly Similar Creatives and Landing Pages</strong></p>
<p>A team received a Meta warning for "low-quality creatives" this week. Further investigation revealed the account had been continuously reusing the same set of ad copy and images for the past two months, only changing CTA button colors. The system identified this as "repetitive creatives" and determined it as inefficient delivery, reducing traffic allocation.</p>
<h2>This Week's High-Risk Operations Checklist</h2>
<p>Based on this week's cases, Yiyu compiled a list of high-frequency risk-triggering operations for account administrators to verify one by one:</p>
<ul>
<li>Changing payment methods more than twice within 72 hours</li>
<li>Using more than 5 similar creatives in a single ad group without carousel settings</li>
<li>Audience size after targeting combinations below 500 people</li>
<li>Landing page load speed exceeding 5 seconds with bounce rate above 70%</li>
<li>Multiple ad accounts sharing the same landing page domain</li>
</ul>
<p>If your account matches any of the above, please check the notification center in your ad backend for pending matters as soon as possible.</p>
<h2>Quick Audit SOP: Complete Account Self-Check in 30 Minutes</h2>
<p>The following are the standardized inspection steps used by the audit team to help you complete a preliminary check in 30 minutes:</p>
<h3>Step 1: Check Notification Center for Status</h3>
<p>Open Meta Business Suite or Google Ads interface, prioritize checking the bell icon in the upper right corner. Ignoring unread notifications is a common mistake among operations staff—at least 3 ban events this week had clear warning notifications 48 hours in advance, but teams missed the remediation window due to delayed checking.</p>
<h3>Step 2: Review Last 7 Days Account Change Records</h3>
<p>Confirm whether the following operations occurred: payment method changes, account permission personnel changes, significant budget adjustments (over 50%), delivery region switches. Operation logs can be viewed in "Account Settings - Activity Records."</p>
<h3>Step 3: Review Active Creatives and Copy</h3>
<p>It is recommended to use Yiyu's creative deduplication tool to check for similarities exceeding 70% with historically delivered creatives. Excessive similar creatives affect the system's evaluation of account quality.</p>
<h3>Step 4: Verify Landing Page Accessibility</h3>
<p>A team reported normal ad clicks but zero conversions this week. Investigation revealed the landing page was inaccessible in some regions. Use Google PageSpeed Insights or GTmetrix to check load speeds and confirm the server IP is not blocked in the target delivery region.</p>
<h2>Next Week's Compliance Recommendations</h2>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Report Payment Changes in Advance</strong>: If you need to change ad payment methods, it is recommended to contact the platform business representative or agent 3 working days in advance to complete qualification updates, avoiding triggering real-time risk controls.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Maintain Creative Iteration Rhythm</strong>: It is recommended to update at least 20% of ad creatives weekly, avoiding long-term reuse of the same content leading to declining quality scores.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Set Minimum Audience Size</strong>: It is recommended that a single ad group's audience size be no less than 1,000 people. This can be achieved by broadening interest tags or removing some targeting conditions.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>For compliance audit assistance, please contact customer service for specialized services.</p>
<hr />
